\description{
Standard R accessors for native many-facet fits. They retain the estimator's
coordinates and limitations; extracting a number does not qualify inference.

Describe the retained ratings at the fitted calibration and saved Person
estimates, without fitting, scoring Persons again or computing covariance.

Returns all facet-level estimates (person and others) in a single
tidy data.frame. Person rows retain their original identifiers; review or
transform them before writing the result outside a controlled analysis
environment.

These methods inspect the separate result returned by
\code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} with \verb{method = "JML", jml_penalty = "jeffreys"}.
The result does not inherit ordinary JML uncertainty or FACETS diagnostics.
}
\details{
\strong{Coordinates.} The default \code{coef(fit)} is a named numeric vector aligned
with both dimensions of \code{vcov(fit)}. These are free coordinates, not every
displayed facet level: anchors and dependent centered coordinates are omitted.
MML/JML names such as \code{Rater[1]} identify positions within parameter blocks,
not literal level names. Steps are free step offsets, slopes are log slopes,
and estimated population variance is on the log-variance scale. Population
regression coefficients remain on their fitted design scale. CML retains its
existing conditional-coordinate names. Use \code{type = "locations"} for all
saved facet locations with their level labels, and \code{summary(fit)} for tables,
readiness, constraints and interpretation. Location signs are the fitted
model's signs; this accessor does not reorient them as severity.
Default \link[=confint.mfrm_fit]{confint()} intervals use these same coordinates;
GPCM positive-slope intervals require explicit \code{parm = "slopes"}.

\code{type = "persons"} returns saved primary Person estimates: MML EAPs and
JML point estimates are different targets. Person posterior SDs are not
structural standard errors and do not occur in \code{vcov(fit)}. CML has no
Person estimates. Free-coordinate extraction refuses corrected or penalized
JML and any fit whose primary estimates are nonfinite: finite optimizer
proxies must not replace primary boundary values. Explicit component
extraction preserves infinite or missing values in the selected column.

\strong{GPCM slope columns.} \code{coef(fit, type = "slopes")} returns the saved
\code{fit$slopes$Estimate}, which retains working values for compatibility; it
does not select \code{PrimaryEstimate} or attach status columns. Read
\code{fit$slopes} before reporting: \code{PrimaryEstimate} is the target's primary
value under \code{ParameterStatus} and \code{PrimaryEstimateBasis}, whereas
\code{OptimizerEstimate} records the numerical value even when no primary
estimate is available. The distinction matters at boundaries and for
limiting fits. Neither a finite working value nor an available primary
value establishes interval eligibility. See \link{fit_mfrm_technical_details}.

\strong{Covariance.} MML \code{vcov()} reuses the same qualified full joint covariance
as \code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}}, including its model/design restrictions and
numerical checks. It may reevaluate information and integration, but does
not refit. An ineligible source remains an error. For an otherwise eligible
ordinary fixed-population RSM/PCM fit, information failure returns an \code{NA}
matrix with \code{unavailable_reason}, \code{checks} and \code{cautions}; default \code{confint()}
retains coefficient estimates and reasons in its \code{diagnostics} attribute
while bounds are missing. No regularized inverse is substituted. If even
one dense \code{NA} matrix exceeds \code{mfrmr.max_information_bytes}, \code{vcov()} errors
with that reason; use the smaller target-level \code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}} result.
Other MML routes retain their separate qualification checks and error rules.
For two-family GPCM, the checked covariance includes free location, step
and log-slope coordinates. Use \code{confint(fit, parm = "steps")} for
separately checked intervals for the expanded step offsets.
Dependent centered steps require their constraint transformation; diagonal
entries alone do not describe all displayed steps. Nominal coverage across
designs has not been established; see \link{mfrmr_gpcm_scope}.
RSM/PCM singleton-category fits can pass the interval-specific information
review while retaining \code{InferenceReady = FALSE}; returned \code{cautions} and
checks explain the small-sample limitation. No count label alone certifies
or invalidates this covariance.
No JML structural-truth covariance is supplied. CML \code{vcov()} reuses saved
conditional information. If CML's finite numerical point passed its
identification, finite-MLE and stationarity checks but information failed,
\code{vcov()} returns an \code{NA} matrix with \code{unavailable_reason}, \code{checks} and
\code{cautions} attributes. \code{coef()} and \code{logLik()} still expose the retained
values; default \code{confint()} supplies missing bounds with diagnostic reasons.
The warning also limits confidence in point accuracy along weak directions;
no regularized covariance or formal inference is substituted.
Free-coordinate covariance is not the covariance
of a selected component vector or of all displayed levels; use
\code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}} or \code{\link[=confint.mfrm_fit]{confint.mfrm_fit()}} for supported transformations.
Comparison-only \code{coef()} returns the declared contrast estimates, retaining
unavailable rows as \code{NA}; its \code{vcov()} is available only for native CML
conditional comparisons, with the same row/column order.

\strong{Likelihood and counts.} \code{logLik()} returns the retained MML marginal or
ordinary JML joint log likelihood, with \code{likelihood_basis} and \code{ICReason}
attributes. Nonunit weights give a weighted objective, not an ordinary
sampling likelihood. The \code{df} attribute is the free parameter count only
when the current information-criterion contract and solution checks permit
its use; otherwise it is \code{NA}, so \code{AIC()} and \code{BIC()} are also \code{NA}.
\code{parameter_count} retains the actual dimension separately. JML and two-family
GPCM do not gain information-criterion eligibility through these accessors.
Corrected JML, penalized objectives and comparison-only fits do not supply
a generic \code{logLik()} here. For estimated-population/one-family GPCM MML,
likelihood qualification may reevaluate solution information.

CML retains its conditional log likelihood and free dimension. Its
\code{nobs} attribute counts all retained Persons, including extreme totals
contributing zero conditional information; \code{informative_persons} records
nonextreme totals separately. This preserves the existing CML method and
is not a new validation of conditional information criteria. Conditional
and marginal likelihoods must not be compared. For MML, BIC uses Persons,
not rating rows or the sum of rating weights. Counts describe the retained
analysis, not omitted input records or an effective sample size under
dependence. Use \code{nobs(fit, type = "ratings")} to count retained ratings.

For CML, adding extreme-score Persons can increase BIC's penalty even when
the conditional likelihood is unchanged. Report both total and informative
Person counts. A sensitivity calculation using the latter is
\code{-2 * as.numeric(ll) + attr(ll, "df") * log(attr(ll, "informative_persons"))},
where \code{ll <- logLik(fit)} and the informative count is positive. Label this
as an alternative penalty convention, not the package's BIC or a validated
model-selection rule; neither count resolves dependence or sparse-design
asymptotics. Keep the same retained data and conditioning basis in comparisons.

\strong{Target.} Probabilities are evaluated at saved primary Person estimates:
MML uses the saved EAP, while JML uses its primary point estimate. Thus
\code{fitted(fit)} is a conditional plug-in expectation. Evaluating probabilities
at an EAP is different from averaging probabilities over a posterior.
For the latter target, use \code{\link[=mfrm_response_diagnostics]{mfrm_response_diagnostics()}} within its stated
model/design scope. These methods do not change \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} or its
legacy diagnostic conventions. Legacy diagnostic helpers use finite JML
optimizer proxies and omit variances at or below \code{1e-10}; the primary
boundary limits and small positive variances here can therefore give
different residuals. Their observation tables retain \code{PersonMeasureBasis},
\code{PrimaryPersonMeasure}, \code{ConditionalVariance} and \code{ResponseReason} to explain
these differences. Do not assume the routes agree at these limits.

For model-score categories \eqn{k}, let \eqn{p_{ik}} be the conditional
probability. The mean is \eqn{\mu_i=\sum_k k p_{ik}}, the variance is
\eqn{V_i=\sum_k (k-\mu_i)^2p_{ik}}, and the Pearson residual is
\eqn{(y_i-\mu_i)/\sqrt{V_i}}. Rating weights affect estimation but are
not multiplied into these per-rating residuals or probabilities. The
variance has no artificial positive floor. A zero variance leaves the
mean/raw residual available but the Pearson residual missing, with a reason.

A positive/negative infinite JML Person estimate with finite structural
calibration has limiting probability one at the maximum/minimum category.
These limits use the primary estimate, not the finite optimizer proxy.
Other unavailable Person estimates leave the corresponding rows missing.
Nonfinite structural estimates, corrected or penalized fits, two-family
GPCM, imported fits, CML and comparison-only fits are explicitly refused.
CML eliminates Person abilities and does not supply this plug-in target.
The supported posterior or corrected-JML routes remain available through
\code{\link[=mfrm_response_diagnostics]{mfrm_response_diagnostics()}}.

\strong{Rows and scale.} Output order is \code{fit$prep$data} order, including repeated
rating events, and excludes records omitted before fitting. Vector names
and probability-matrix row names are retained-row positions. The \code{rows}
attribute records these positions and original input-row
positions where retained metadata permit recovery; unknown positions are
\code{NA}. No response is silently added or dropped during extraction.
The separate \code{identifiers} attribute retains Person/facet columns in the
same order, including user-supplied facet names that coincide with \code{Row}.

Values use the modeled score scale (\code{InternalScore} in \code{score_map}), as in
ordinary plug-in diagnostics. With collapsed categories this differs from
the original score labels. The saved \code{score_map} attribute retains that
mapping; probability columns name the modeled categories. Inspect the map
before comparing fitted values with an original, unrecoded data column.

\strong{Interpretation.} These same-data summaries hold calibration and Person
estimates fixed. They omit their estimation uncertainty and are not held-out
forecasts, calibrated goodness-of-fit tests or evidence of rater quality.
Point calculation does not establish convergence or inference readiness.
Read \code{source_readiness} and row-specific \code{diagnostics} before interpreting
values, and keep these attributes when saving the result.

\code{Facet} and \code{Level} jointly identify a row; \code{Facet = "Person"} identifies
ability estimates. Use both keys when joining diagnostics: the same level
label can occur in different facets. This compact table omits uncertainty
and estimate-use restrictions; retain the source fit and read \code{summary(fit)}.

See \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} for the penalty, assumptions and restrictions.
\code{checks$ordinary_nll} is the ordinary negative log likelihood evaluated
at the penalized point; \code{checks$objective} also includes the information
penalty. Neither is an ordinary maximized likelihood for AIC or testing.
\code{category_support} describes observed category usage, including missing
categories; its ordinary-MLE warnings are not penalty-rank diagnostics.
}
\section{Interpreting estimates}{

\code{Estimate} is on the fitted latent scale, not the raw rating scale. With
default orientation, larger Person values indicate higher ability, larger
Rater values greater severity, and larger task/criterion values greater
difficulty. Non-Person facets in \code{positive_facets} have the opposite score
direction. Interpret values relative to the fitted centering/anchors;
zero is a scale reference, not absence of ability or severity.

In an additive RSM/PCM with default signs, a Rater difference of 0.5 lowers
adjacent-category odds by a factor \code{exp(-0.5)} for the same Person and other
facet levels. It does not subtract 0.5 raw-score points. GPCM slopes and
fitted interactions modify that comparison; use \code{\link[=predict.mfrm_fit]{predict.mfrm_fit()}} for
response-scale predictions at specified facet combinations.

Person \code{Estimate} is an EAP under MML, conditional on fitted calibration
and the specified population model; under ordinary JML it is the fitted
Person parameter. EAP shrinkage affects the spread of these estimates, so
their empirical SD is not the fitted population SD. \code{Extreme} describes
observed all-minimum/all-maximum scores, not a statistical outlier test.
An extreme score need not give an infinite MML EAP. For uncertainty use
\code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} and, for supported structural contrasts,
\code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}}; a missing interval does not erase an available point.
}

\examples{
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_core")
fit <- fit_mfrm(ratings, "Person", c("Rater", "Criterion"), "Score",
  method = "MML", quad_points = 31, maxit = 500)
coef(fit, type = "locations") # Labelled facet locations, including constraints
coef(fit)                     # Independent coordinates, including steps
nobs(fit)                     # Persons contributing to marginal likelihood
nobs(fit, type = "ratings")    # Retained response rows
ll <- logLik(fit)
attr(ll, "ICReason")          # Read before interpreting AIC/BIC
AIC(fit)
# When covariance is qualified, its names match the free coefficients:
# V <- vcov(fit); identical(rownames(V), names(coef(fit)))
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_core")
fit <- fit_mfrm(ratings, "Person", c("Rater", "Criterion"), "Score",
  method = "MML", quad_points = 31, maxit = 500)
expected <- fitted(fit) # Expected ratings at saved EAP abilities
head(data.frame(Observed = fit$prep$data$Score, Expected = as.numeric(expected),
  Residual = as.numeric(residuals(fit))))
head(attr(expected, "diagnostics")) # Check unavailable and zero-variance rows
attr(expected, "settings")         # Read the target and interpretation limits
head(fitted(fit, type = "prob"))    # One column per modeled score category
# Optional workflow; run the example explicitly as shown below.
# example("as.data.frame.mfrm_fit", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
library(mfrmr)

# Saved synthetic MML/RSM analysis of example_operational.
# For your own data, fit a model first; see ?fit_mfrm.
example_fit <- readRDS(system.file("examples", "operational-mml.rds", package = "mfrmr"))
fit <- example_fit$fit

# Extract estimates and select the rows to display
estimates <- as.data.frame(fit)
head(subset(estimates, Facet == "Person")) # First six persons
subset(estimates, Facet == "Rater")       # All raters
subset(estimates, Facet == "Criterion")   # All criteria
}
# Long-running workflow; run explicitly when needed.
# example("summary.mfrm_jeffreys_fit", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_core")
# Each row is one Person's rating by a Rater on a Criterion.
fit <- fit_mfrm(ratings, "Person", c("Rater", "Criterion"), "Score",
  method = "JML", jml_penalty = "jeffreys", rating_min = 1, rating_max = 4,
  category_policy = "preserve")
summary(fit)$persons # Finite penalized Person points, without SEs
summary(fit)$checks$max_gradient # Penalized score-equation diagnostic
plan <- unique(ratings[c("Rater", "Criterion")])
scores <- fair_average_table(fit, reference_plan = plan)
scores$reference_scores # Expected averages under the same plan for all Persons
intervals <- confint(scores, parm = c("P001", "P002"), method = "delta")
intervals[, c("Target", "Estimate", "SE", "Available", "CIEligible")]
# Numerical availability is not a qualification of small-sample coverage.
}
}
